WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
INTRO:
If you’ve ever used a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.200
card to pay for something, you’re definitely&nbsp;
familiar with the names American Express,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.880
Visa, and Mastercard. These three have dominated&nbsp;
the card processing industry for decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.520
but most people don’t know the difference between&nbsp;
them. And frankly, there’s very little difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.640
between them from the consumer side if you’re not&nbsp;
keen on maximizing credit card points and rewards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.600
However, from the business side, American Express&nbsp;
is radically different from the other two.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.680
American Express is not the most common card&nbsp;
by any means; in fact, Visa and Mastercard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.280
destroy Amex in terms of issued cards. For&nbsp;
example, Visa has 343 million credit cards in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:43.520
circulation within the US and 798 million credit&nbsp;
cards in circulation outside the US. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.840
Mastercard has 249 million credit cards in the&nbsp;
US and 725 million credit cards outside the US.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.640
This means that Visa has a total of 1.14&nbsp;
billion credit cards in circulation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:01.120
Mastercard has 974 million cards. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
Amex only has 53.8 credit cards in the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.560
and 58.2 million cards outside the US,&nbsp;
or just 112 million cards in total.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:12.320
This means that Visa and Mastercard have issued 20&nbsp;
times as many cards as Amex. At first, this looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.000
really bad for Amex until you take a look at their&nbsp;
revenue numbers. In the last 12 month period, Visa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.080
pulled in $24.1 billion in revenue and Mastercard&nbsp;
pulled in $17.8 billion in revenue. Together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:31.440
this adds up to $41.9 billion. Meanwhile, Amex&nbsp;
pulled in $41 billion in revenue by themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.000
Now, this isn’t an exact science or anything,&nbsp;
but if we divide each of their revenues by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.840
number of cards in circulation, we’ll see that&nbsp;
each Amex customer is worth $366 in revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:48.000
Meanwhile, each Visa customer is only worth $21&nbsp;
and each Mastercard customer is only worth $18.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.080
Now, this already paints a terrible picture&nbsp;
for Visa and Mastercard, but we haven’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.280
considered the elephant in the room. Here’s the&nbsp;
thing, Amex doesn’t really deal with debit cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.040
so the vast majority of their revenue truly comes&nbsp;
from just credit cards. The same, however, cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.920
be said about the other two. Visa and Mastercard&nbsp;
are heavily involved in the debit card game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:16.480
Visa has a total of 2.4 billion debit cards in&nbsp;
circulation and Mastercard has 1.4 billion debit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.760
cards in circulation. So, really, each issued&nbsp;
card is worth less than $10 in annual revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:28.560
for Visa and Mastercard meaning that each Amex&nbsp;
customer is 36 times as valuable. So, here’s how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:35.840
Amex became the king of credit cards and why high&nbsp;
spenders far prefer Amex over Visa and Mastercard.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.240
HUMBLE ROOTS:
Taking a look back, Amex wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:45.440
always the high ticket closer that they are today.&nbsp;
In fact, they weren’t even a financial business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.560
they were originally a shipping business.&nbsp;
It all started on March 18, 1850, when three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.840
men named Henry Wells, William Fargo, and John&nbsp;
Butterfield decided to join forces. Together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.120
these guys owned three shipping businesses&nbsp;
that they had been running for a few years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:05.760
The first company was called Livingston, Fargo, &amp;&nbsp;
Co and was founded in 1845 by Henry and William.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.520
The second company was called Wells &amp; Co and was&nbsp;
founded in 1846 by Henry. And finally, the third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.800
company was called Butterfield &amp; Wassan and was&nbsp;
founded in 1849 by John Butterfield and James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:21.760
Wassan. At the time, railroads were a rather new&nbsp;
invention, and the demand for sending shipments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.560
was growing rapidly. Instead of competing against&nbsp;
each other and seeing who came out on top though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:30.400
the trio decided to combine their efforts and&nbsp;
form a small monopoly within New York called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.360
American Express. The company mainly focused on&nbsp;
shipping goods between New York City and Buffalo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.720
but they also had some destinations throughout the&nbsp;
midwest. This proved to be an extremely profitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:46.320
regional business, but neither Henry nor William&nbsp;
were satisfied with just this market. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.680
these two wanted to build a national business, so&nbsp;
they wanted to expand to California and capitalize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.720
on the gold rush. However, the other board&nbsp;
members would not approve this expansion effort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.560
Henry and William weren’t willing to give up&nbsp;
this opportunity though, so they would start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:06.160
a new express and financial services business in&nbsp;
California in 1852. I think it’s safe to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.920
Amex missed out pretty big here given that the new&nbsp;
business was Wells Fargo. Nonetheless, things were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.560
going well for Amex as well. They were expanding&nbsp;
into the money order business and their monopoly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.520
was still intact up until 1866 when they saw their&nbsp;
first major competitor called Merchants Union&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:26.640
Express Company. Amex and Merchants Union fought&nbsp;
fiercely for market share over the next two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.360
but in 1868, they decided to simply join&nbsp;
forces and create American Merchants Union&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:36.720
Express Company. Honestly, that’s quite a long&nbsp;
and cumbersome name, but fortunately, they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.680
shrink the name back to American Express in&nbsp;
1873. They had successfully avoided a long-term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.040
clash with Merchants Union, but this fiasco made&nbsp;
them realize the importance of diversification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.480
And with that, Amex would start to explore&nbsp;
new paths throughout the next few decades.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.240
FINANCIAL SERVICES:&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.520
Amex started off by thinking of new&nbsp;
ways they could build customer loyalty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.640
and provide more value. Many of Amex’s customers&nbsp;
were small local businesses that were using Amex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:07.120
to source their goods. These local businesses&nbsp;
didn’t have the resources to constantly travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.720
to their suppliers and negotiate better prices.&nbsp;
But, Amex already had agents all over the place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.120
So, in 1878, Amex started negotiating&nbsp;
with suppliers on behalf of their clients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.680
and this has turned into one of the trademarks&nbsp;
of Amex today. Of course, they’re no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.720
negotiating purchase orders; instead, they’re&nbsp;
negotiating for high-demand items and experiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:32.480
No one knows exactly how Amex pulls it off, but&nbsp;
if you’re a platinum cardholder or better yet a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.040
centurion cardholder, Amex can get you anything&nbsp;
you want whether that be front row seats to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:42.480
Lakers game or an invitation to Justin Bieber’s&nbsp;
wedding. Anyway, going back to the 1870s, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.040
new negotiation service was a massive hit with&nbsp;
customers, and Amex would follow up this success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.920
with the Traveler’s check in 1891. If you’re not&nbsp;
familiar with what a traveler’s check is, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:56.720
basically just a safer way to carry money with you&nbsp;
while you’re traveling. Instead of carrying cash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.120
you carry a traveler’s check and redeem it for&nbsp;
money when you arrive at your destination. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.120
was particularly appealing to international&nbsp;
travelers who regularly traveled to Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.440
This transformed Amex into an international&nbsp;
business, and they would open offices in England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.920
Germany, and Paris. Soon after, World&nbsp;
War 1 rolled around, and Amex would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:18.000
score a deal with the British government.&nbsp;
Amex was contracted to deliver letters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.640
money, and relief packages to British prisoners&nbsp;
throughout the war. And by the end of the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:27.760
Amex was delivering 150 tonnes of parcels every&nbsp;
single day to just prisoners. It looked like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:33.840
things couldn’t get any better, but then the&nbsp;
American government would screw over Amex.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.840
THE GREAT WINTER:
Throughout the winter of 1917,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:42.320
the US would experience a massive coal shortage&nbsp;
which meant that it became extremely expensive to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:47.920
ship items across the US. This wasn’t optimal for&nbsp;
Amex, but given that shipping rates had gone up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.560
across the board, Amex didn’t really have to worry&nbsp;
about losing customers to competitors. They did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:58.400
however, have to worry about the government.&nbsp;
On December 26, 1917, President Woodrow Wilson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.800
ordered the railroads to help in the war effort&nbsp;
by helping them move troops, supplies, and coal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.880
and all contracts between railroad companies&nbsp;
and express companies were nullified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.560
While this was an unfortunate turn of&nbsp;
events, Amex couldn’t really complain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.520
given that the decision was made for a much more&nbsp;
important cause. Wilson’s next decision, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:19.680
was definitely something worth complaining&nbsp;
about. Treasury Secretary William McAdoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.400
proposed the idea of consolidating all existing&nbsp;
express companies into a nationalized company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.680
President Wilson approved this proposal, and with&nbsp;
that Amex lost their express business. All express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:33.920
companies were hurt, but Amex was hurt the most&nbsp;
given that they were the largest express company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:39.280
They lost 10,000 offices, 30,000 employees,&nbsp;
and their rights to 71,000 miles of railroad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.240
The new nationalized company was called American&nbsp;
Railway Express Agency, and though the US would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:49.360
return ownership to the private companies in&nbsp;
1920, it would never be the same. Amex had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:54.240
lost everything that was unique to them. They had&nbsp;
given up a highly respected personalized express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:02.160
service, and in return, they basically got a 40%&nbsp;
stake in USPS. And no one likes USPS. In 1929, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.880
assets and operations of American Railway Express&nbsp;
would be transferred to Railway Express Agency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:11.360
and that was basically the end of this business.&nbsp;
The company declined for decades until it finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:16.400
filed for bankruptcy in 1975. I guess it was&nbsp;
a good thing that Amex had sort of diversified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.920
into traveler’s checks, but this didn’t help much.&nbsp;
For the US, the great winter only lasted a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:26.480
of months, but for Amex, it would end up lasting&nbsp;
41 years until they stumbled upon credit cards.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:27.040
CREDIT CARDS:&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:35.280
Amex had considered the possibility of&nbsp;
launching a credit card as early as 1946,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.440
but it wasn’t until the launch and success of the&nbsp;
Diners Club card that Amex decided to take the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.960
plunge. At the end of 1957, Amex announced that&nbsp;
they were going to enter the credit card business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.760
From the very beginning, Amex was set on targeting&nbsp;
the premium market and capturing high spenders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:55.920
They charged an annual fee of $6 which was $1 more&nbsp;
than the Diners Club’s $5 fee. Amex figured that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.320
this would make their card seem more exclusive and&nbsp;
desirable, and they were completely right given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:05.360
the massive demand that followed. By the time&nbsp;
the official launch happened in October of 1958,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:12.480
Amex had already issued 250,000 cards. Initially,&nbsp;
the cards were printed on paper, but in 1959, Amex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.280
introduced plastic cards with embossed numbers.&nbsp;
Similar to how everyone wants a metal card today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:23.040
back in 1959, everyone wanted Amex’s new plastic&nbsp;
card. Amex further built on this exclusive image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.800
with the Gold Card in 1966 and the Platinum&nbsp;
Card in 1984. The Platinum card was invite-only,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:33.120
and Amex only picked the highest spenders with&nbsp;
the best payment histories. Aside from being hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:39.120
to qualify for, the Platinum card carried a hefty&nbsp;
annual fee of $250 which has since grown to $695.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:43.760
And to persuade people to pay these hefty fees,&nbsp;
Amex introduced their famous Membership Rewards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:49.040
program in 1991. Within just 10 years Membership&nbsp;
Rewards became the world’s largest card-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:54.480
rewards program, and Amex has held that title&nbsp;
ever since. Aside from building an exclusive image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:59.200
amongst customers, Amex was concurrently building&nbsp;
exclusive relationships with merchants. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:03.200
offered lower merchant fees to restaurants and&nbsp;
businesses if they exclusively accepted American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:08.640
Express. This worked pretty well between 1981&nbsp;
and 1991, but a group of merchants would start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:14.080
rebel against Amex in 1991. Instead of exclusively&nbsp;
accepting Amex, they started to exclusively accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:19.440
Visa and Mastercard. And with that Amex would back&nbsp;
off with their exclusivity deals, but they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:24.960
never give up their exclusive image.
AMEX TODAY:&nbsp;

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.280
Even today, exclusivity is the name of the&nbsp;
game when it comes to Amex because their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:33.040
business model is radically different from&nbsp;
the rest of the industry. A lot of credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.080
card companies make their money on&nbsp;
the high-interest rates they charge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:41.600
And given that 55% of Americans carry a credit&nbsp;
card balance, this is quite profitable. But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.240
model would never work for Amex because they only&nbsp;
issue cards to customers with excellent credit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:51.520
And the thing is, people with excellent credit&nbsp;
don’t carry balances. Amex is well aware of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.520
and this is why many of their cards don’t&nbsp;
even allow customers to carry a balance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:00.400
The Amex Green Card, Gold Card, Platinum Card,&nbsp;
and Centurion Card are all charge cards which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:05.280
means that you have to pay your balance in full&nbsp;
every month. Amex has become more lenient over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:09.840
the past few years, and introduced workarounds&nbsp;
that allow you to sort of carrying a balance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.960
but this is by no means their focus. In that case,&nbsp;
you might think that their annual fees are their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:19.840
focus. Amex cards often carry annual fees in the&nbsp;
hundreds of dollars and even thousands of dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:24.080
but while this pulls in a lot of revenue,&nbsp;
it’s not exactly that profitable. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:28.000
sourcing tickets to Justin Bieber’s wedding and&nbsp;
giving customers access to Centurion airport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.560
lounges aren’t exactly cheap. And, given&nbsp;
that their customers are smart spenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:37.600
most of them are not gonna pay a $5000 annual fee&nbsp;
unless they’re confident they can milk $6000 worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:42.880
of value. But then why does Amex spend so many&nbsp;
resources on what seems like a zero-sum or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:48.240
negative-sum service? Well, the answer is that&nbsp;
you are their partner, and they’re willing to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:53.920
to extreme lengths to keep you happy. You see, the&nbsp;
key to Amex’s business is not high-interest rates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:59.760
or high annual fees, the key is the high fees they&nbsp;
charge merchants. Visa and Mastercard only charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:07.040
1.25 to 2.5% per transaction, but American&nbsp;
Express charges 1.5 to 3.5% per transaction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:12.240
This may not sound like much, but an extra&nbsp;
1% on every transaction adds up super fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:17.200
Because of this, most merchants aren’t a fan of&nbsp;
Amex, but they’re forced to accept Amex because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:22.000
all the high spenders prefer to use Amex. And&nbsp;
this creates a super unique business dynamic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:26.720
Visa and Mastercard partner with retailers to&nbsp;
try to get you to spend as much money as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:31.680
and pay as much interest as possible. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
Amex partners with you to try to get as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:36.480
retailers to accept their high fees as possible.&nbsp;
And so far, they’ve been extremely successful at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:41.840
this given that 99% of US merchants who accept&nbsp;
a credit card also accept American Express. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:45.600
how about we keep up this streak and continue&nbsp;
supporting American Express. Do you guys have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:50.320
an Amex card? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if you’re a fan of Amex’s business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:53.840
model. And of course, consider joining&nbsp;
our discord community to suggest future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.960
video ideas and consider subscribing to&nbsp;
see more questions logically answered.

